servatives  as i am  even though i am probably more  reasonable than some of the  conservatives that they go after  somebody else i mean i  assume you studied the way people even  treat you now  people often say you're a contradiction  they write it all the time this  great success in foreign policy and  but the difficulties you had in your  presidency alone how do you address that  the contradictions in richard nixon or  do you think about it  well i really don't think about it but  since you asked the question i would say  that that's uh that's part of a  political life  i have  i have read a lot as you know  i repeat what i said in our first  interview that  i  don't consider history ripe  until it's 50 years old so i read a lot  about the 19th century and early earlier  periods in which democracies began to  develop in  britain and the united states and so  forth  and  you find that leaders in democratic  countries are generally  not universally liked all the time  they they they are very effective in  some areas not as ineffective in other  areas  in my case  i was considered to be by most  somewhat effective in foreign policy  although many disagreed with me on  the fact that i insisted on  trying to win peace with honor in  vietnam  uh  on the other hand  they uh felt that uh politically that uh  i was uh not the kind of a leader that  they liked and i understand for example  in the watergate period that that would  certainly be justified but it wasn't  just that it was in other areas as well  as you're well aware  what about watergate and vietnam what  are the  you know  those two periods uh difficult as they  were for the country what's the legacy  of that  those periods and uh  you see any in the youth  well  the legacy of watergate  i mean and vietnam fortunately now is  pastors the syndrome is over  and it was particularly see vietnam more  than watergate  uh vietnam  has been  described by many  who  are are frankly not not really aware of  what happened or if they are aware  they're they're not properly disclosing  it  vietnam was not a military defeat for  the united states  every american had left vietnam two  years before it fell  and it fell because the congress didn't  provide the assistance to the south  vietnamese that were our allies that the  soviets and the chinese were providing  to the north vietnamese  but it was an enormous defeat for the  american spirit because a country that  we had supported  two years after we had left  it came under communist domination  and so right after vietnam  the general  conventional wisdom in this country was  that the united states couldn't do  anything well because we had lost in  vietnam to this third  world  power  uh that was wrong  just as it was wrong to assume  after we had won in the persian gulf  that we we could do anything  after vietnam we could do nothing and  after we  won in the gulf we could do anything and  then of course to carry the syndrome a  little further after the collapse of  communism  in russia and eastern europe there's  nothing left to do  these are all instant evaluations which  are incorrect the world remains the same  the united states still  could play a major role after vietnam  because we had not suffered a military  defeat  and we should have played the role  for a while we did not  and it cost us dearly  and the same is true with regard to  these other areas at the present time to  say that because communism is collapsed  in the the former soviet union that  there's nothing left to do that assumes  that that's all the problems we had let  me just give you one example  people will be surprised by this but  since the end of world war ii there have  been 140 wars  eight million more people have been  killed in those wars than were killed in  all of world war one which used to be  called the big one  most of those wars would have been  fought had there been no conflict  between the soviet union and the free  world and the united states  now that has not changed that is why the  united states must continue  to maintain adequate military forces  because what happened in the gulf  affecting our vital interests can happen  in other places as well  back to a couple of quotes saint thomas  aquinas  if the highest aim of a captain were to  preserve his ship he would keep it in  port forever  that quote incidentally came from  a book by lon fuller lon fuller  uh my professor at duke university  gave a series of of lectures at yale  and that was a i in reading that i found  the quote i would like to say that i  read everything that saint thomas  aquinas had written but i didn't i got  it out of there  and i think it's a marvelous quote  because what he is what the quote tells  us that seven centuries ago  that  an individual  must not be satisfied with just keeping  what he has many conservatives  make the mistake of saying what we're  going to have to do is to keep what we  have we mustn't risk anything  in order to be better or to improve  and as you point out  if you if the whole the sole purpose of  a leader or of an individual is to  retain what he has  uh then  that's the same as uh keeping a boat in  in  because of your uh  unwillingness to take it to sea  in other when you take that boat out to  sea it's true you're going to run into  rough weather sometimes  sometimes it may even be sunk  but if  what you do is simply keep it in the  harbor forever because of your fear  of what the world out there holds then  you're not going to be what you really  should and can be  working up to the limit of your  capabilities you told us in our first  hour that  if you had to pick a city you'd go back  to where you liked the most would be  istanbul  if you had to pick a book  a book that you  enjoy reading the most maybe reading it  again you say you'd like to go back and  read them over again what would that  book be  well i would answer and i can say this  in a way that it doesn't certainly  appear to be political uh you would have  to say take the bible for that i mean  the bible apart from religion is great  literature  the new testament particularly and even  parts of the old testament i've always  felt that the book of ecclesiastes for  example was some of the most eloquent  writing  uh that  that i've ever seen and it's uh  it always lifts you  so i would i would pick that as a book  now  among others  very hard to tell among current authors  that i read who write historically i  would say  the books by paul johnson appealed to me  a great deal he's a he's a marvelous  writer he's a geopolitical thinker  uh  the  and many people of course remember his  later books but the one that is my  favorite is is one on the british the  offshore islanders it's just a marvelous  book it's it's not only great prose it's  great poetry as well  if you have to pick music that you want  to listen to the most what would you do  uh not vocal music i'm uh i'm not one  who goes much for opera perhaps because  i don't like the uh the social part of  opera you know i've been invited many  times and supposed to dress up and you  go to the dinners and all that sort of  thing  uh  i uh  i think that i would prefer the the  tchaikovsky  ballets i don't like ballet particularly  but and i've seen swan lake a number of  times i've seen it believe it or not  i've seen swan lake in moscow i've seen  it in leningrad i sent it in spurs lost  i have seen it also in nova scotia and  all of them with great companies but if  if i had to turn on  a record i would like that  some of the tchaikovsky  ballets and so forth  and i like liszt i know that somebody  was saying to me once when i wanted some  number the lids preludes played at one  of our  inaugurations the leader of the  orchestra didn't want to play it because  he said hitler liked it oh my goodness  the fact that hitler may have liked the  music doesn't mean that  i that i liked it because he did  lists preludes has a moving quality to  it t